{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Warren1968-T5-HLHHLH-x4-a200",
  "Source": "S. W. Warren, \"Properties and performance of basic designs of infrared interference filters,\" Infrared Phys. 8, 65-78 (1968). DOI not printed in the paper - Table 5, p. 73",
  "Notes": "Bandpass of 4 basic unit(s) HLHHLHS with every layer absorbing (alpha = 200 cm^-1), 23 layers; per-layer absorption loss\nSpecification: Absorption loss at lambda0/lambda = 1, lambda0 = 4 um.\nReported: Table 5 (p. 73): total absorption 37.3 % of incident radiation at lambda0 = 4 um. Absorption loss per layer, layer 1 (air side) to 23 (substrate side), in %: 0.57, 2.38, 4.58, 2.35, 0.55, 0.88, 0.51, 2.15, 4.15, 2.13, 0.49, 0.77, 0.43, 1.8, 3.47, 1.78, 0.41, 0.67, 0.39, 1.65, 3.18, 1.63, 0.37.\nNotes: Design string written out: HLHHLHLHLHHLHLHLHHLHLHLHHLHS. Table 5 numbers layers 1 = next to air, same as here. Both H and L absorb with alpha = 200 cm^-1 (k = 0.006366 at 4 um, constant). Absorption is in the films only; the back surface of S does not change film absorption apart from a tiny second pass, which the 1968 numbers appear to ignore. H = quarter-wave optical thickness of index 4.0, L = quarter wave of index 1.8 at lambda0, S = substrate of index 1.65 (p. 66); adjacent equal letters (HH, LL) are merged into one half-wave layer of 2 QWOT. The paper writes designs from the incident (air) side to S, so the order is kept as printed. lambda0 is not fixed for Tables 1-3 and the figures (results are in lambda0/lambda); 4000 nm (4 um, as in Tables 4-6) is used, which is harmless because all indices are constant. Recomputations against the paper match only if the uncoated back surface of the n=1.65 substrate is included incoherently (substrate -> air): air/L/1.65 alone gives T = 89.4 % at lambda0 while Table 4 prints 84.6 %, and with the back surface it is 84.6 %; the rejection minima of Sec. 2.1.3 (3.45 %, 0.087 %, 0.0020 %, 0.000047 %) also match exactly only with the back surface. Normal incidence, unpolarised.\nLayers from Table 5, p. 73; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours A 38.6 % at 4 um; paper total absorption 37.3 % (Table 5).",
